the data right now for for our human  brain samples is available for download  but the story will be available  hopefully by the end of this month so so  stay tuned and definitely check that  story out i'm excited about that one too  okay  another question  that came in is from carmen with royal  college of surgeons in ireland  is there a difference in rna quality  when using ffpe versus fixed frozen  can you test rna quality prior to probe  collection  yeah um so so geomets works with ffpe  tissue fixed frozen  the  the striatum samples from the mouse  brain that we profiled for fresh frozen  so we have protocols for  all three tissue preparation types we we  do not see big differences in rna  quality um well in signal quality  from section to section with our geomex  whole transcriptome readout  sample type  the sample type with that readout and  then if you want to test the the quality  of your samples prior to probe  collection  our our assay is in nc2 hybridization  based assay so we really recommend that  you use  like a lower plex  ish assay to test the quality in such as  rna scope look at medium and low  expressors and if you can detect medium  and low expressors in your tissue of  interest you should be good to go with  using the geomex rna assays  great thank you another question that  came in is from gabriel at lcsb um did  you sort the cells for iba 1 if yes are  you missing some cells that are in the  amygdala but don't necessarily express  the marker  yeah so so  in in this specific experiment we did  not collect from the iba1 positive  cells uh so we looked specifically at  neurons  um and then the neural pill component we  have done experiments where we've looked  at microglia and have  used the combination of ibm iba1 and  team m119  for the that type of segmentation and  actually there is some microglia  specific segmentation coming in the  human story  great a couple more questions from  gabriella um  did you take into consideration patch  and matrix in the interior posterior  differences  yeah so  um  i'm not sure i don't think i ended up  including a full  image of one of those  sections that we profiled from the  striatum but essentially the same  coronal plane was used for every single  mouse so we matched the anterior  posterior position from mouse to mouse  to mouse  that were profiled there and and again  really focused on the  the dorsolateral dorsal medial and  ventrolateral components of the striatum  great another question from gabriella  how does this data compare to humans  since the structure of the striatum  differs between mice and humans  is a fantastic question so i would  really love to see some geomex data um  from human striatum to answer that  question right now i don't i don't have  a great answer i think we would really  like to see the the comparable  data from human  make a call from that  great and another question that came in  from mark  you mentioned having a human brain  transcriptive atlas as well  did you or others see any significant  differences  as have been recently shown in drg  sensory ganglia by ted price and others  yeah so for our human brain  transcriptome atlas we profiled areas of  the  cortex and hippocampus  specifically so we don't have as many  regions profiled  within that data set but we do go a  little bit deeper into the differential  expression between the different cell  types and the different given areas  all right and another question from  carmen when drawing contours  is it possible to do this in the geomix  software or does the file need to be  exported into image j  what file type ome tiff or is tiff  sufficient  yeah so we have a  contouring type script for image j that  can be used  with our software to  draw the contours so  the file type that you'll export there's  actually a specific export button  in within the user interface of the  software specifically  for  exporting the roi specific tiff files so  you don't need the ome tiff  for that  you export individual tip files run the  script import them directly back  into the software and then you can  actually visualize the the contours  directly  on your sample before you collect so if  you wanted to go back and do any  additional editing and image j  you could do that you'll see exactly  what will be collected  excellent and then we have another  question from gabriella that just came  in what is the collection protocol for  fresh frozen samples how do you  dissociate the tissue my concern is  always that i miss some cells that are  more sensitive to damage  yeah so so i mean one of the nice things  about the the genomics workflow is that  it's it's non-destructive to your tissue  so  um we do  fix the tissue  we do fix fresh frozen tissue actually  overnight before we proceed with our  sample preparation which is a pretty  standard nc2 hybridization  preparation  and ms leaves  the tissue really nicely intact you can  adjust your proteinase concentration  kind of depending on your concerns of  for your specific sample type but  in our experience that initial fixation  is robust and allows  moving forward with the processing of  most stable types  excellent well thank you so much allison  so um does anyone else have any more  questions that they didn't get a chance  to answer um now is the time  all right well that concludes our q a  session for today's webinar  um do you have any more final comments  for our audience allison  um i don't think so thank you all so  much for your your time and attention  it's it's really exciting to get to  share some of this  neurospecific geomets data with the  greater community  thank you  so thanks everyone for your time today  and thanks alison for presenting this  research and before we go i'd like to  thank the audience for joining us today  and further engaging questions  questions that we didn't have time for  today and those submitted during the  on-demand period  will be addressed by the speaker via the  contact information you provided at the  time of registration and this webcast uh  will be available on demand for two  years um until march of 2024  um labyrinths will alert you via email  when it's available for replay and we  encourage you to share that email with  your colleagues who may have missed  today's live event and that is it and  that concludes the nana sharing webinar  for labyrinth neuroscience 2022 thanks  everyone and we'll see you next time  thank you  you

